     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 9,000 mg/day of acetaminophen is about 128 mg/kg/day for a 70 kg patient. That is in the range where hepatotoxicity is expected, and I don't know of any indication that justifies it. Repeated doses at this level can cause liver injury that is hard to reverse.\n\nI can enter one of these right away:\n- Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO three times daily (3 g/day)\n- Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO four times daily (4 g/day, the standard maximum)\n- Acetaminophen 650 mg PO every 6 hours\n\nIf the 3000 mg figure was a typo, for example for 1000 mg, tell me and I'll enter the corrected dose. If you need more analgesia than these give, I can help enter a multimodal regimen, such as an NSAID if appropriate or an opioid. If you have a specific reason for the higher dose, please talk to pharmacy or toxicology first."
      }
     ],
